sections.s2_technical:
The classic concerns are uniqueness, lazy creation, and thread safety. The cleanest and safest approach in Java is the enum singleton or the initialization-on-demand holder idiom, which is lazy and thread-safe without explicit locking because class loading is synchronized by the JVM.

```java
class ConfigManager {
    private ConfigManager() {}
    private static class Holder { static final ConfigManager INSTANCE = new ConfigManager(); }
    static ConfigManager getInstance() { return Holder.INSTANCE; }
}
```

The naive double-checked locking requires a volatile field to be correct, and a plain eager static field is simple but creates the instance even if never used. A connection pool is the archetypal singleton because opening connections is expensive and the pool must be shared and coordinated; multiple pools would exhaust the database.

The senior nuance is that Singletons are easy to overuse and become hidden global state that complicates testing. I prefer dependency injection to hand out a single managed instance, which gives the "one instance" benefit while keeping the code testable with mocks. A true Singleton is justified only for genuinely process-wide resources.

